@page
@model CSharpMasteryHub.Pages.AspNetCoreRestApis.RoutingAndParametersModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Routing & Parameters";
}

<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="lesson-content">
                <h1 class="display-4 mb-3">Routing & Parameters</h1>
                <div class="d-flex gap-3 mb-4">
                    <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">20 min read</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-primary">Lesson 7 of 10</span>
                </div>

                <div class="alert alert-primary">
                    <i class="fas fa-map-signs me-2"></i> <strong>The Mail Carrier Story:</strong> A mail carrier needs a specific address to deliver a letter. <code>MyStreet</code> isn't enough; they need <code>MyStreet, House #5</code>. In APIs, the URL is the street, and the Parameters are the house numbers that pinpoint the exact data you want.
                </div>

                <h2>Connecting to C#</h2>
                <p>Think about C# methods. If you want a specific user, you pass an ID to the method:</p>
<pre><code>public User GetUser(int id) { ... }</code></pre>
                <p>In a REST API, we do the exact same thing, but the ID comes from the URL!</p>

                <h2>Types of API Parameters</h2>

                <h3>1. Route Parameters (The Address)</h3>
                <p>Passed directly in the URL path. Best for identifying a specific resource.</p>
<pre><code>// URL: https://api.myapp.com/users/5
[HttpGet("{id}")]
public IActionResult GetUser(int id)
{
    // id will be 5
    return Ok($"You requested user {id}");
}</code></pre>

                <h3>2. Query Parameters (The Filter)</h3>
                <p>Added after a question mark <code>?</code>. Best for searching, sorting, or filtering.</p>
<pre><code>// URL: https://api.myapp.com/products?category=electronics&maxPrice=500
[HttpGet]
public IActionResult GetProducts(string category, decimal maxPrice)
{
    // category = "electronics", maxPrice = 500
    return Ok($"Searching {category} under ${maxPrice}");
}</code></pre>

                <h3>3. Body Parameters (The Package)</h3>
                <p>Used in POST and PUT requests. The data is hidden inside the HTTP request body, not the URL. Best for sending large, complex C# objects.</p>
<pre><code>// The client sends a JSON object in the body
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult CreateUser([FromBody] User newUser)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically converts the JSON to a C# User object!
    return Ok($"User {newUser.Name} created!");
}</code></pre>

                <div class="mt-5 d-flex justify-content-between">
